  <h2 style="margin-top:0">What the surviving record indicates</h2>
  <p>Historic captures and external references associate “The Spy Guitar” with a sampled-instrument project previously published on this domain. The available record is sufficient to explain why someone may still follow the old name here. It is not sufficient to establish that the current publisher owns the recordings, instrument file, text or artwork.</p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <div><dt>Current publisher</dt><dd>Matthias Ramahi, from August 2026</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Former authorship</dt><dd>Not claimed by the current publisher</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Download</dt><dd>Not offered</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Rights status</dt><dd>No redistribution right established</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <h2>Why there is no replacement download</h2>
  <p>Recreating a filename or hosting a copy found elsewhere would not solve the rights question. It could also expose readers to a modified or unsafe file. The archive therefore records the absence instead of presenting a dead or misleading button.</p>

  <h2>A current route to the same kind of learning</h2>
  <p>The new guide <a href="/sound-design/sampled-instruments/building-a-sampled-instrument/">Build a small sampled instrument in Ableton Live</a> explains a workflow using source audio the reader records or is licensed to use. It is original editorial work and is not a reconstruction of The Spy Guitar.</p>

  <h2>Sources used for this record</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/*/http://auspiciousmusic.com/" rel="noopener">Internet Archive capture index for the former domain</a></li>
    <li>surviving external backlink and anchor records reviewed during the 2026 relaunch audit</li>
  </ul>
